  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 PM EDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nThe big story continues to be the impacts from the remnants of \nHurricane Isaias for this weekend. Rain and wind will begin to ramp \nup overnight tonight. High winds are likely by daybreak in the \nmountains and foothills. While the low of the storm tracks through \nmiddle TN the impacts will expand far from the center of the system. \nBottom line: it's going to be a wet and windy weekend for the \nTennessee Valley, so make sure you're weather aware and prepared!\n\nHere are the more specific impacts from the remnants of Hurricane \nIsaias for Saturday and Sunday:\n\nHeavy Rain Potential: some tropical bands of heavy rain may move \nacross the Plateau (especially southern), southeast Tennessee, and \nsouthwest North Carolina. Upslope flow into the Plateau will aid \nwith the lift and potential banding precipitation on Saturday. \nRainfall amounts of 1 to 2 inches are possible. Again, downslope \nwinds will limit QPF amounts over the northern and central valley.\n\nA flood watch remains in effect for parts of southeast Tennessee and \nsouthwest North Carolina.\n\nHigh Winds Potential: Increased confidence for gusty winds this \nweekend. High Wind Warning issued for Mountains, foothills. Wind \nAdvisory issued elsewhere. Strong gusty winds are likely!\n\nThe main corridor of high winds will be along the eastern TN \nmountains and foothills. The hi-res suite continues to depict the \npotential of high winds with peak gusts up to 70 mph in the \nmountains on Saturday. The tight pressure gradient from the wedge \nover the Carolinas and Isaias low pressure will produce strong \nwinds. Mountain wave high winds look likely for the far east \nTennessee mountains and foothills. A High Wind Warning goes into \neffect tonight through Sunday morning to address the potential high \nwinds. We did expand the High Wind Warning to include Washington and \nRussell counties in SW VA. I have adjusted the High Wind Warning and \nWind Advisory start time to begin at midnight EDT/11pm CDT as winds \nare expected to ramp up quickly. \n\nFor Chattanooga area, the threat of gusts in excess of 60 \nmph has diminished some, however, gusts of 50-60 mph remain \npossible, especially Lookout and Signal mountains. \n\nFor the rest of east Tennessee, southwest Virginia, and southwest \nNorth Carolina: Surface winds of 15 to 30 mph with gusts up to 50 \nmph are possible Saturday and Sunday. We have opted to issue Wind \nadvisories to address the strong gusty winds.\n\nSevere Thunderstorm potential: The potential for severe weather \nlooks quite limited. Instability is low and mainly elevated \nespecially for Saturday and Saturday night as the warm sector \nremains to our south and east. A few rumbles of thunder cant be \nruled out but severe weather is not expected. \n\nLooking ahead to next week, we will see the return of upper level \nridge as it builds into the region at the start of the week. This \nwill return a prolonged stretch of dry, mostly sunny weather for \nmuch of next week. High temperatures will run 5 to 10 degrees above \nnormal.",
